      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "description",
      "value": "Design and execute a numerical experiment to test the Geometry (Low-Dimensional Manifold) hypothesis regarding network width scaling. Steps: 1) Define a target function family on a low-dimensional manifold (e.g., 2D sinusoidal surface in 3D space) to generate constraint samples; 2) Vary constraint count w (sample size) and network width N (hidden neurons); 3) Train small feedforward networks with early stopping; 4) Compute maximum absolute error on held-out constraints; 5) Identify N_c(w) as the minimum width achieving zero violation (error ≤ 1e-3); 6) Plot N_c vs w to determine if N_c is constant (rigid) or grows. SKILL: None (custom ML workflow). FILES: workspace/experiments/ml_scaling/test_n_scaling.py, workspace/experiments/ml_scaling/scenario_def.py. PREDICTION: If the Geometry hypothesis holds, N_c(w) will plateau at a small constant value (e.g., N_c ≈ 16-32) as w increases beyond a threshold, showing no logarithmic or linear growth. SLOTS: w_values=[10, 50, 100, 500, 1000]; N_range=[8, 16, 32, 64, 128, 256]; error_threshold=1e-3; target_function=2D_manifold_sin; network_arch=3-layer_ff; optimizer=Adam; loss=MSE."
    },
